me the nhs had the  equivalent review  of what you actually do you mentioned in  passing  that gp work is fundamentally redefined  anita talked about  the need to think about a fundamental  new worker a type of worker somewhere  between  care worker and nursing in the nhs but  doesn't the nhs  need to have the equivalent of a defense  review if it's to keep going  and taking more money from the treasury  extent  that is what we saw in the london plan  it didn't do everything  it did do some elements of rethinking  what that model  would look like and that's where some of  the changes in general practice are  coming from  i think what is valid though is that the  world has moved on because of  covid both the challenges the nhs is  facing but also the level of ambition  about  how quickly the images can change i  think  like many people who work with the nhs  for over many years  sometimes the pace of change whether in  new technology or new services  sometimes seems to be slower than people  would hope but what we've seen over the  last  months since march is sometimes changes  at breathtaking speed and i think we're  about to see it again on the vaccination  program  what it's shown is if you get the  ingredients right  the nhs can change at enormous pace i  think it's that ambition  we need to come back to you to think  again for the next few years let me put  the same questions to you anita but  adding in that point that richard  brilliantly made there at the end  that that you can uh  be cleverly inventive whether you're  dealing with the vaccination program  under  nadeem zahawi or whether you're talking  about at oxford university working with  astrazeneca or whatever it might be  but you've got to bank those changes  you've got to bank those experiences and  not simply move on  to the next phase one of the things that  we see across the nhs  time and time again is pockets of  outstanding innovation  and people uh pushing forward and doing  incredible things wh