ade  it so difficult when covid hit  to manage that well to sustain other  services and when we look across  different countries  countries that have taken the biggest  economic hit are countries that have  taken the biggest health here  so the treasury you know kind of it's  the economy  or its health argument is utterly false  you know a really well functioning nhs  that's really efficient  it's not that it's  our public finances you know it's part  of being a successful global economy  the final point before i bring danny in  to comment upon what  what both you and richard have said but  but let me press you anita  on on the very point that richard was  making  gps have learned to do things in a  different way  uh force major they had to because  because of kovid 19  but but they have now become more  accustomed to using  laptops pads smartphones etc etc etc  that's more cost effective as well but  it isn't necessarily what  a 68 year old woman who's been widowed  once  uh or a young mom who's living on her  own bringing up a child  if that is the way forward and it saves  money  it requires a huge education program as  well because we pay the bills  absolutely although quite interestingly  the one service  that for over a decade has been entirely  phone uh or online is actually the  pension service  yeah so the first government service  that closed its offices  i'm not suggesting gps close their  office by the way but it is a paradox  that we do talk a lot about and digital  literacy is really important and i  i'm not but it's about the support you  provide  and it can't be just digital and we are  slightly in field hospital mode at the  moment you know we've had to  we've had to innovate very rapidly and  we do need to make sure that we learn  from that innovation  phase but we do actually very carefully  now adapt  i mean people are talking about this  we're all sat in our bedrooms or  whatever at the moment home working  most people are saying i don't want to